          Pick a penetrating concrete stain if the slab is bare, sound and reasonably attractive, if moisture is a concern (basements and slabs on grade), or if you never want to scrape peeling paint, because stain soaks into the surface and cannot peel. Pick paint if you want a solid, uniform color, need to hide patches, oil marks or mismatched concrete, or want a bright, easy-to-mop surface, and accept that a film on concrete will eventually chip and need recoating. For a garage floor that takes daily car traffic, neither is the best choice, and a two-part epoxy usually beats both. The rest of this page explains why, factor by factor, with a side-by-side table to make the call quickly.
        </p>

        <div id="table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Concrete Stain vs Paint: The Side-by-Side
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before the details, it helps to see the two finishes next to each other. When people say concrete stain they usually mean one of two penetrating products: acid stain, which reacts chemically with the concrete, or water-based penetrating stain, which carries fine pigment into the pores. Paint means a film-forming coating, most often a latex, acrylic or one-part epoxy-acrylic porch and floor paint. The table compares those two families on the things that actually decide the choice.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Penetrating stain (acid or water-based)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Floor paint (acrylic or 1-part epoxy-acrylic)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">How it bonds</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soaks into or reacts with the surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Forms a film on top of the surface</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Translucent, variegated, concrete still shows</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Opaque, uniform solid color</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hides flaws</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, often highlights them</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, covers stains and patches</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Can it peel?</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The stain cannot; its sealer can wear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, if prep or moisture is wrong</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tolerance for slab moisture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Better, especially with a breathable sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor; vapor pressure causes blistering</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Needs a sealer?</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, for protection and sheen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, the paint is the finish</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical recoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reseal every 1-3 years outdoors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Repaint every 2-5 years by traffic</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical DIY materials</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.50-$1.50 per sq ft with sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.30-$0.80 per sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Works over old paint or sealer?</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, needs bare porous concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, over sound, compatible coatings</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The single most useful row is the one about bonding. A stain becomes part of the concrete's top layer. A paint is a separate skin glued to it. Nearly every advantage and disadvantage of each product follows from that. Stain is limited by the concrete it lands on, since it can only color what is already there. Paint is limited by its bond, since it can only last as long as it stays stuck.
          </p>

          <p>
            One trap deserves a mention at the top. Some products sold as solid color concrete stain are, chemically, thin acrylic coatings. They behave much more like paint than like a penetrating stain: they hide the surface, form a film and can peel. Read the label. If it describes the product as opaque, solid or film-forming, treat it as paint for every decision on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Do Concrete Stain and Paint Actually Work?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Acid stain is a water-based solution of metallic salts with a mild acid, commonly hydrochloric acid. When it is applied to concrete, the acid opens the surface slightly and the metal salts react with the calcium hydroxide, or lime, that forms as cement cures. The reaction produces colored compounds inside the concrete itself. The result is permanent color in the surface layer, typically in earthy browns, tans, terracottas and blue-greens, with a marbled, uneven quality that no two slabs reproduce exactly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based penetrating stain does not react. It is a thin acrylic or polymer carrier loaded with very fine pigment that soaks into open pores and dries there. Because the color comes from pigment rather than chemistry, the palette is much wider, including grays, blacks and modern neutrals, and results are more predictable from one slab to the next. It still lets the texture and character of the concrete show through, just more evenly than acid stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paint works on a completely different principle. A concrete floor paint is a pigmented binder that dries into a continuous film sitting on the surface. Good floor paints contain acrylic or epoxy-modified resins chosen for abrasion resistance and adhesion to masonry. The film is what you see and what takes the wear. It needs something rough and clean to grab, which is why preparation, etching and primers dominate the paint instructions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both stain types need a clear sealer on top for most uses. The stain gives the color, and the sealer protects that color from abrasion, stains and weather and adds the sheen. Our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete sealer guide</a> covers the choices, and the trade-off between an acrylic film sealer and a breathable penetrating one is explained in our <a href="/acrylic-vs-penetrating-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">acrylic vs penetrating sealer</a> comparison. Paint, by contrast, is normally its own finished surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="looks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Looks Better, Stain or Paint?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It depends entirely on the concrete you start with. Stain is translucent, so it amplifies what is already there. A smooth, consistent, unpatched slab with a pleasant texture can look rich and expensive after acid staining, often compared to leather or natural stone. A slab with oil spots, tire marks, grout smears, patch repairs or drywall mud will show every one of those through the stain, often more clearly than before.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paint does the opposite. It gives you one flat, controllable color that hides most cosmetic problems: mismatched concrete from an addition, old repair patches, rust marks and general grime. If the goal is a clean, bright, uniform basement or a porch that matches the house trim, paint does that job and stain cannot. Light colors in particular are only practical with paint, since stain darkens the concrete rather than lightening it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect surprises with acid stain. The color depends on the cement content, the age of the slab, how it was finished and how the stain reacts locally. Test it on a hidden spot or a leftover piece of concrete first. Water-based stain is more predictable and can be layered or blended to adjust tone. If you want a decorative, natural-stone effect on a patio, our guide on <a href="/how-to-stain-concrete-patio-to-look-like-stone/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">staining a concrete patio to look like stone</a> walks through the technique.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sheen is mostly a sealer decision for stained floors and a product choice for painted ones. A glossy sealer over stain deepens the color dramatically but makes scratches and dust more visible. Satin floor paint hides wear better than gloss and is the usual pick for basements. Either way, keep in mind that a glossy finish on a smooth slab can be slippery when wet, which matters on patios and pool decks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Lasts Longer, and Can Concrete Stain Peel?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A true penetrating stain cannot peel because there is no film to lift. The color sits in the top fraction of the concrete itself. What wears is the sealer on top, and the color under it can slowly abrade in high-traffic paths if the sealer is allowed to wear through. With routine resealing, the color can last for many years. Without sealer, a stained exterior slab fades and scuffs much faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paint lasts only as long as its bond. On a well-prepared, dry slab with a quality floor paint, a basement floor can look good for several years and a covered porch for a few. On a poorly prepared or damp slab, paint can chip, blister or peel within a season. Garage floors are the hardest case: hot tires, road salt and automotive fluids attack the film, and one-part paints commonly show wear in the tire paths in a year or two. Our guide to <a href="/why-is-my-concrete-floor-paint-peeling/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">why concrete floor paint peels</a> covers each failure mode.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance looks different too. A stained floor is maintained by cleaning it and recoating the clear sealer, often every one to three years outdoors for acrylic sealers and longer indoors. Resealing is fast and forgiving. A painted floor is maintained by touching up chips and eventually repainting, which means cleaning, scuff-sanding and rolling the whole floor again. Repainting over sound paint is easy; repainting over peeling paint means scraping or grinding first.
          </p>

          <p>
            The long-run difference shows up when you change your mind. Paint can be painted over in a new color, or stripped back to concrete with effort. Acid stain is effectively permanent, and you cannot lighten it later. You can paint over a stained and sealed floor if you remove or properly prepare the sealer, but you cannot stain over paint without removing all of it first, because stain needs open, bare concrete to soak into.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Stain fades slowly; paint fails suddenly.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A stained floor wears gradually and is refreshed with a coat of sealer. A painted floor looks perfect until its bond fails, then it chips or peels and needs real work to fix.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Handles Moisture Better?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Moisture is often the deciding factor on slabs that sit on the ground. Concrete is porous, and slabs without a good vapor barrier underneath can pass water vapor up from the soil continuously. A film of paint on top can trap that vapor. Pressure builds under the film, and the result is blisters, a white powdery residue under the paint and eventually peeling. Basement floors and older garage slabs are the usual victims.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating stain plus a breathable sealer tolerates this far better. Because there is no continuous film, vapor can pass through the surface and evaporate. Penetrating silane or siloxane sealers repel liquid water while letting vapor escape, which suits damp-prone slabs. A thick acrylic or epoxy film sealer over stain can reintroduce the same trapping problem, so the sealer choice matters as much as the stain choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test before you decide. Tape a square of clear plastic, about 18 by 18 inches, to the slab on all sides and leave it for 16 to 24 hours. Condensation under the plastic or darkened concrete means significant moisture is moving through. The method is explained in our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-moisture-test/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete moisture test guide</a>. If the test is wet, paint is a gamble, and a stain with a breathable sealer is the safer finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stain is not a cure for water problems. If water actually seeps through cracks or pools on the floor, or the slab has white mineral deposits from efflorescence, the cause needs fixing first. Efflorescence can also interfere with acid stain reactions and sealer adhesion. Our guide to <a href="/efflorescence-on-basement-walls/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">efflorescence</a> explains what those white deposits mean and how to deal with them before any finish goes down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Needs More Prep, and Which Costs More?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both need clean, bare, porous concrete, so prep is closer than people expect. For stain, the slab must be free of sealers, curing compounds, paint, glue, oil and wax, because any of those will block absorption and show as blotches. Sprinkle water on the slab: if it soaks in and darkens the surface within a minute or so, stain can penetrate. If it beads, something is sealing the surface and needs to come off first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paint needs the same cleanliness plus a surface profile. Smooth, troweled concrete is often too dense for paint to grip, so most labels call for etching with an acid or citric etcher, or light grinding, to give the surface a texture similar to medium sandpaper. Paint can go over old sound paint after cleaning and scuff-sanding, which makes repainting easier than restaining. Acid stain has an extra step at the end: neutralizing the acid residue and rinsing thoroughly before sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            On cost, DIY paint is usually cheaper. Typical DIY materials for floor paint, including degreaser, etcher, a primer where needed and two coats of paint, run very roughly $0.30 to $0.80 per square foot. A stain job adds the cost of the sealer on top of the stain, so DIY materials often land around $0.50 to $1.50 per square foot. Prices vary by brand and region and change over time, so treat these as rough planning numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hiring out reverses the picture somewhat. Professional painting of a concrete floor commonly runs in the low single digits per square foot, roughly $2 to $5 including basic prep. Professional single-color acid or water-based staining with sealer often lands in a similar or somewhat higher range, and decorative multi-color staining costs more. Our detailed breakdown of <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-paint-a-concrete-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what it costs to paint a concrete floor</a> covers room sizes and hidden costs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Should You Stain or Paint a Basement Floor, Patio or Garage?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Basement floors</h3>

          <p>
            Basements are where stain usually wins. Slabs on grade below ground are the most likely to pass moisture, and a stain with a breathable sealer avoids the blistering and peeling that plague painted basement floors. Stain also gives an unfinished basement a deliberate, finished look without hiding the concrete. Choose paint if the slab is visibly patchy, if you want a bright light color, and the moisture test comes back dry. Our <a href="/best-basement-floor-paint/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement floor paint picks</a> focus on products that tolerate basement conditions best.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Patios and porches</h3>

          <p>
            Outdoors, stain has the edge on uncovered slabs. Sun, rain, freeze-thaw cycles and standing water are hard on paint films, and a peeling patio is a frequent, frustrating sight. A penetrating stain resealed every couple of years keeps color with less drama. Covered porches are a fairer fight, and porch and floor paint is a traditional, attractive choice there. For exterior stain recommendations, see our <a href="/best-outdoor-concrete-stain/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">outdoor concrete stain guide</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Garage floors</h3>

          <p>
            Garages are where both fall short for daily parking. Stain under a standard acrylic sealer does not stand up well to hot tires and oil; paint is vulnerable to hot-tire pickup and chemicals. If you park every day, a two-part epoxy or polyaspartic system is the more durable answer, and our <a href="/garage-floor-paint-vs-epoxy/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garage floor paint vs epoxy</a> comparison explains the trade-off. For a workshop garage with occasional parking, a quality one-part epoxy-acrylic paint on a well-prepared slab is a reasonable budget option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, the result will only be as good as the prep. If you go with paint, follow our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-paint-a-concrete-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to paint a concrete floor</a>, and if you go with stain, the same principles apply: clean, bare, dry concrete, tested before the first coat. The products themselves matter less than most buyers think, and the slab matters more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining over a sealed slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stain has to soak into open pores. Over an old sealer, curing compound or paint residue, it sits on the surface, blotches or wipes away. Do the water test first: if drops bead instead of soaking in and darkening the concrete, strip or grind the sealer before staining, or choose paint instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting stain to hide flaws</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stain is translucent. Patches, oil spots, tire marks and old glue lines will show through, and acid stain can make them more obvious. If the slab is visibly blotchy and you want a clean, uniform look, paint or a solid-color coating is the right product, not a penetrating stain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting a damp basement floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moisture vapor rising through a slab on grade is the leading reason painted basement floors blister and peel. Tape down a plastic sheet for at least 16 to 24 hours before you buy paint. If it shows condensation or darkened concrete, choose stain with a breathable sealer or fix the moisture first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the sealer on stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stain colors the concrete, but on its own it gives little protection against abrasion, stains or weather. Unsealed stained floors scuff and fade faster, especially outdoors and in traffic paths. A compatible sealer is part of the system, and it needs to be recoated on a schedule to keep protecting the color underneath.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not neutralizing acid stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acid stain leaves an acidic residue that must be neutralized and rinsed off before sealing. Skipping it can leave a powdery layer that stops the sealer bonding, causing it to cloud or peel. Follow the label for neutralizing, rinse until the water runs clear, and let the slab dry fully before applying sealer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over stained concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you prepare it. A stained floor usually has a sealer on top, and paint will not bond well to it. Strip or scuff the sealer, clean thoroughly, and use a bonding primer if the paint label calls for one. Test adhesion on a small area first. Remember that going back from paint to stain later means removing all the paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain concrete that has been painted?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not directly. Stain needs bare, porous concrete to soak into, so all the paint has to come off first, typically by chemical stripping, grinding or both. Even after stripping, residue in the pores can cause blotches. If the paint is sound and you just want a new color, repainting is far less work than converting a painted floor to stain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is concrete stain more slippery than paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The stain itself does not change traction much; the sealer or paint finish does. Glossy sealers and glossy paints on smooth concrete can both be slippery when wet. For patios, steps and pool areas, choose a satin or matte finish and add a non-slip grit additive to the final coat of sealer or paint, following the manufacturer's directions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does concrete stain fade in the sun?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Acid stain is generally UV stable because the color comes from a chemical reaction in the concrete, though the surface can wear in traffic. Some water-based stains may fade gradually outdoors depending on the pigments used. In both cases, a UV-resistant sealer slows wear. Paint can also fade or chalk in full sun, especially in darker colors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does stained concrete last compared with paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The stain color itself can last for many years because it is in the concrete. The protective sealer typically needs recoating every one to three years outdoors and less often indoors. Painted concrete usually needs repainting every two to five years depending on traffic, sun and moisture, and much sooner if the original prep or slab moisture was a problem.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is acid stain safe to use indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be used indoors with precautions, but it contains acid and releases fumes. Ventilate well, protect walls and anything metal nearby, wear chemical-resistant gloves, eye protection and a respirator if the label recommends one, and keep children and pets away. Water-based penetrating stains are a gentler indoor option with lower odor and easier cleanup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for a garage floor, stain or paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For light use, a well-applied one-part epoxy-acrylic floor paint is usually more practical because it resists oil spots visually and cleans easily. For daily parking, neither is ideal: hot tires lift paint, and standard sealers over stain wear quickly. A two-part epoxy or polyaspartic coating is the more durable choice for a working garage floor.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

